# Site configurations

Every site the user hands over gets an entry in `sites/sites.json`, shown read-only in the app's **Sites** panel. The server matches a queued URL's host against `match` (domain or subdomain) and passes `ytDlpArgs` to yt-dlp; `sites/plugins` is always passed with `--plugin-dirs`.

When given a URL, establish success yourself:

1. Probe without downloading: `curl -I`, then `yt-dlp --simulate` (full network permission).
2. Prove it with real media: download the file or a sample into a scratch folder under `/tmp` (never `~/Downloads`), check it with `ffprobe` and a full `ffmpeg -f null` decode, then delete it.
3. If it fails, fix in this order: update yt-dlp, per-site options (`ytDlpArgs`), the user's browser cookies (ask first), a recipe, and only if no recipe can express it, a plugin in `sites/plugins/siphon/yt_dlp_plugins/extractor/` (yt-dlp ignores a `yt_dlp_plugins` folder placed directly in the plugin dir). The entry's `plugin` path is relative to `sites/plugins`.
   - Built-ins first: a site recipe starts from a built-in (`"base": "followEmbed" | "smartScan" | "playerConfig"`, defined in `Recipe.swift`) and sets only the fields that differ. If no built-in fits but the technique could apply to other sites, add a new built-in (and a learner attempt) rather than a one-off recipe; check that the learner finds the site with no entry. Fields set only for polish (e.g. `title`) should be dropped if the built-in's default already gives the same result.
   - The goal is as many recipes and as few plugins as possible. When a site needs something recipes can't do yet, prefer extending the recipe engine with a general capability over writing a plugin; keep plugins for real code (computed signatures, multi-step APIs).
   - A `recipe` is a JSON object the native app (`mac/Siphon/Engine/Recipe.swift`) runs before yt-dlp: `fetch` (`auto`, the default: plain request, falling back to `BrowserFetch`, yt-dlp impersonating desktop Chrome, on a 403/503 or Cloudflare challenge; `browser`: always use it), `embed` (`match`: regex for the player iframe's src, or omit to pick the most player-like iframe; `referer`: fetch it with the page as referer, default true), `unescape` (undo `\"` `\/` `\u0026` JavaScript escaping), `media` (`ytdlp`: give yt-dlp the player URL; `sources`: read `<source>`/`<video src>` and give yt-dlp the tallest that fits the Quality setting; `pattern`: give yt-dlp the URL captured by `pattern`, a regex with one group, e.g. from a player config in a script), `title` (regex with one group, applied to the page), `mediaReferer`. It only fetches and reads text; nothing from the site runs.
   - Retired plugins go to `sites/plugins-archive/` with restore steps in its README.
4. Record the entry: `method` (`builtin` | `options` | `recipe` | `plugin` | `unsupported`; following a player iframe is the recipe `{"embed": {}, "media": "ytdlp"}`, and older `"method": "embed"` / `"followEmbed": true` entries are converted to it when the app loads them), `status` (`working` | `broken` | `unsupported` | `untested`), `lastTested`, `testResult` (what the check showed), `notes` (why it failed and why the fix works), `exampleUrl`.
5. Do not circumvent DRM; mark such sites `unsupported` and say why.

## The app's persistent copy

- The native app keeps its own copy at `~/Library/Application Support/Siphon/sites/` (`sites.json` + `plugins/`), seeded from the bundled repo copy and merged on every launch: a repo entry replaces the entries it overlaps unless one of them has a later `lastTested`.
- When a link's site has no entry, the app tests it during the real download, tries automatic fixes (browser impersonation, referer, generic page scan, following the page's player iframe, reading the player's video sources), checks the file with `ffprobe`, and records the result with `"source": "learned"`.
- When a built-in recipe works, the learner records it with what the download showed filled in (`SiteLearner.specialized`): the player iframe's host as `embed.match`, the player-config key that held the URL as `pattern`, and a title trim checked on the page (from `og:site_name` or the domain) as `title`. Learned sites are retested on every download, so stale details fall back to the plain built-ins and are re-recorded. `fetch: browser` is deliberately not learned (the default `auto` is faster when plain requests usually pass).
- The Sites window points out player hosts shared by two or more working sites; those are candidates for a new built-in, which still needs writing and checking by hand.
- Learned `broken` entries are the work list: fix them with the steps above (custom recipes, plugins and cookies are beyond the app), then record the fix in the repo's `sites/sites.json` with a newer `lastTested` so it wins the merge. Promote useful learned `working` entries the same way.
